By default, RStudio does not contain any files in `~/.R/snippets` (#84). If the snippets of some kind (e.g., SQL) were not changed in RStudio through the default snippets editor window accessible through "Top menu in RStudio → Tools → Global Options → Code → Edit Snippets" the files with snippets are not created. And if one tries to edit snippets for the first time by using `usethis::edit_rstudio_snippets(???)` creates an empty `???.snippets` (??? - the kind of snippets) file and, in this way, it deletes the default RStudio snippets. E.g., `usethis::edit_rstudio_snippets("sql")` deleted the default snippets for SQL on my PC (there were several default snippets before):
![snippets](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/12725868/64559513-264d7b80-d34f-11e9-96a7-fc677fdbbdde.png)

The behavior of `edit_rstudio_snippets()` should be safer: 

1) it either should open a file and create the default snippet if they are not present;
2) or open the default snippets editor window in which the default snippets are already present (I'm not sure if **rstudioapi** allows programmatically accessing this functionality at the present moment);
3) or at least warn the user that the default snippets will be lost and allow to cancel the operation.

